  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
    "lesson_core_move": "Derive the one-sided Laplace transform of $u(t)$ as $U(s)=1/s$ by treating it as the $a=0$ case of $e^{-at}u(t)$ and checking the $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$ convergence condition.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The target object in this step is the one-sided Laplace transform of the unit step. You should recognize that $u(t)$ is the special case of $e^{-at}u(t)$ when $a=0$, so this section reuses the same transform structure from the previous lesson and narrows it to the simplest base signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the one-sided transform setup directly from the definition: start with the time-domain signal, attach the exponential kernel, and keep the $0^-$ lower limit so the setup is valid for causal signals in one-sided Laplace notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $u(t)=1$ for $t\\ge 0$, the integral reduces to a plain exponential integral over the one-sided interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate once to get $U(s)=\\left[-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\right]_{0^-}^{\\infty}=\\frac{1}{s}$, and then require the convergence condition $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$ so the upper-limit term vanishes. This gives the unit-step pair result, which we will reuse throughout later examples in this lesson.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Computing the Unit Step Transform as the $a=0$ Exponential Case",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the one-sided Laplace transform of $x(t)=u(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The key cue is that this is the same one-sided exponential-pattern transform from the previous section, with the new wrinkle $a=0$. Use the standard causal definition and write the transform directly from the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For $t\\ge 0$, $u(t)=1$. Replace the signal with that constant and simplify the setup to an exponential-only integral over the one-sided interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate, then evaluate the antiderivative at the bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-st}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\right]_{0}^{\\infty} = \\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-sb}\\right)+\\frac{1}{s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Require $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$ so the term $e^{-sb}$ decays to zero as $b\\to\\infty$, leaving only $\\frac{1}{s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\frac{1}{s},\\quad \\operatorname{Re}(s)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "State the transform pair as $u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{s}$, and note that this is explicitly a one-sided result with lower limit $0^-$ because we are using the unilateral definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Deriving $U(s)=1/s$ from the One-Sided Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,dt$ and obtain the one-sided transform $U(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this as a bound-checking verification pass. The cue is still the unit-step transform, but now the goal is to make the one-sided limit handling explicit so it can be reproduced step by step.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}u(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $u(t)$ is one on $t\\ge 0$, the integral is the same as evaluating the antiderivative of $e^{-st}=e^{-(s+0)t}$, with the lower limit kept as $0^-$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}e^{-st}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}\\right]_{0^-}^{\\infty}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate the lower bound first: at $t\\to 0^-$, $\\lim_{t\\to 0^-}e^{-st}=1$, so the lower-term contribution is $-(-1/s\\cdot 1)=1/s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now evaluate the upper bound with a parameter $b$ to isolate convergence: write $\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}e^{-st}\\,dt=\\lim_{b\\to\\infty}\\left(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-sb}\\right)+\\frac{1}{s}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For convergence the first term must vanish; this happens only when $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$. If $\\operatorname{Re}(s)\\le 0$, the upper expression does not decay and the one-sided integral does not produce a finite transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "U(s)=\\frac{1}{s},\\quad \\operatorname{Re}(s)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the final reusable statement as $u(t)\\leftrightarrow 1/s$ with explicit ROC tag $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$, which is the form students will reuse directly in the next practice question model.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Checking the $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$ Convergence Condition",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $U(s)=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} e^{-st}\\,dt$, determine where the integral converges.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a convergence check rather than a new integration. Keep the same expression from the previous step and test when the upper-limit term decays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=\\sigma+j\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute into the exponential to isolate decay: $e^{-st}=e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}$. Only $e^{-\\sigma t}$ controls the magnitude as $t\\to\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|e^{-st}\\right|=\\left|e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\right|=e^{-\\sigma t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate the limit at infinity: if $\\sigma>0$, then $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}e^{-\\sigma t}=0$ and the improper integral term vanishes. If $\\sigma\\le 0$, the term does not decay, so the one-sided integral is not finite.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "State the region of convergence as a required part of the pair knowledge, not a decoration: $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$, i.e. $\\sigma>0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{ROC: }\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Carry this forward by attaching the same condition to the stored pair $u(t)\\leftrightarrow 1/s$, so later inverse lookup and inverse-check questions remain mathematically valid.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of the Unit Step",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $1/s$ as the Unit Step Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Match $U(s)=1/s$, $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$, to its one-sided time-domain signal and explain the match.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is inversion-retrieval: use the computed $U(s)$ and convert it back to the time-domain signal family from this lesson, keeping the ROC as part of the answer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from nearby causal pairs in this sequence: $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=1/s$ with $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>0$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{e^{-at}u(t)\\}=1/(s+a)$ with $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>-a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Reject nonmatching forms by checking the denominator pattern and ROC. A shifted exponential like $1/(s+1)$ is not $1/s$, and missing or opposite-sided ROC also disqualifies the exact unit-step match.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The exact pair is therefore",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "u(t) \\leftrightarrow \\frac{1}{s},\\quad \\operatorname{Re}(s)>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Record this as a reusable inverse rule: $\\frac{1}{s}$ with right-half-plane ROC is the causal unit step, while $\\operatorname{Re}(s)$ tag errors (for example $\\operatorname{Re}(s)<0$) should be flagged before selecting an answer.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
